1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,050
Nowadays, we use different products for personal cleanliness, laundry,

2
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:09,462
dishwashing and household cleaning,

3
00:00:09,462 --> 00:00:13,140
but this is very much a 20th-century development.

4
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:18,038
The origins of cleanliness date back to prehistoric times.

5
00:00:18,038 --> 00:00:21,270
Since water is essential for life,

6
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:27,530
the earliest people lived near water and knew something about its cleansing properties –

7
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:31,100
at least that it rinsed mud off their hands.

8
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:35,250
During the excavation of ancient Babylon,

9
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:41,810
evidence was found that soapmaking was known as early as 2800 BC.

10
00:00:41,810 --> 00:00:46,690
Archaeologists discovered cylinders made of clay,

11
00:00:46,690 --> 00:00:51,270
with inscriptions on them saying that fats were boiled with ashes.

12
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:55,964
This is a method of making soap, though there's no reference to the purpose

13
00:00:55,964 --> 00:00:56,970
of this material.

14
00:00:56,970 --> 00:01:03,420
The early Greeks bathed for aesthetic reasons and apparently didn't use soap.

15
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:09,990
Instead, they cleaned their bodies with blocks of sand, pumice and ashes,

16
00:01:09,990 --> 00:01:12,092
then anointed themselves with oil,

17
00:01:12,092 --> 00:01:17,980
and scraped off the oil and dirt with a metal instrument known as a

18
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:18,400
strigil.

19
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:21,780
They also used oil mixed with ashes.

20
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:26,050
Clothes were washed without soap in streams.

21
00:01:26,050 --> 00:01:32,688
The ancient Germans and Gauls are also credited with discovering how to make a

22
00:01:32,688 --> 00:01:34,110
substance called 'soap',

23
00:01:34,110 --> 00:01:37,501
made of melted animal fat and ashes.

24
00:01:37,501 --> 00:01:41,860
They used this mixture to tint their hair red.

25
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:49,568
Soap got its name, according to an ancient Roman legend, from Mount Sapo, where

26
00:01:49,568 --> 00:01:51,220
animals were sacrificed,

27
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:53,510
leaving deposits of animal fat.

28
00:01:53,510 --> 00:01:59,835
Rain washed these deposits, along with wood ashes, down into the clay soil along

29
00:01:59,835 --> 00:02:01,190
the River Tiber.

30
00:02:01,190 --> 00:02:07,530
Women found that this mixture greatly reduced the effort required to wash their clothes.

31
00:02:07,530 --> 00:02:09,901
As Roman civilisation advanced,

32
00:02:09,901 --> 00:02:11,680
so did bathing.

33
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:15,830
The first of the famous Roman baths,

34
00:02:15,830 --> 00:02:21,940
supplied with water from their aqueducts, was built about 312 BC.

35
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:24,318
The baths were luxurious,

36
00:02:24,318 --> 00:02:27,290
and bathing became very popular.

37
00:02:27,290 --> 00:02:30,520
And by the second century AD,

38
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:37,630
the Greek physician Galen recommended soap for both medicinal and cleansing purposes.

39
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:47,757
After the fall of Rome in 467 AD and the resulting decline in bathing

40
00:02:47,757 --> 00:02:48,480
habits,

41
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:53,200
much of Europe felt the impact of filth on public health.

42
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:59,513
This lack of personal cleanliness and related unsanitary living conditions were major factors in

43
00:02:59,513 --> 00:03:03,120
the outbreaks of disease in the Middle Ages,

44
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:07,270
and especially the Black Death of the 14th century.

45
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:12,290
Nevertheless, soapmaking became an established craft in Europe,

46
00:03:12,290 --> 00:03:17,030
and associations of soapmakers guarded their trade secrets closely.

47
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:23,509
Vegetable and animal oils were used with ashes of plants, along with perfume, apparently

48
00:03:23,509 --> 00:03:25,360
for the first time.

49
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:31,430
Gradually more varieties of soap became available for shaving and shampooing,

50
00:03:31,430 --> 00:03:33,980
as well as bathing and laundering.

51
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:41,210
A major step toward large-scale commercial soapmaking occurred in 1791,

52
00:03:41,210 --> 00:03:48,109
when a French chemist, Nicholas Leblanc, patented a process for turning salt into soda

53
00:03:48,109 --> 00:03:48,602
ash,

54
00:03:48,602 --> 00:03:50,080
or sodium carbonate.

55
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:57,593
Soda ash is the alkali obtained from ashes that combines with fat to form

56
00:03:57,593 --> 00:03:58,130
soap.

57
00:03:58,130 --> 00:04:04,280
The Leblanc process yielded quantities of good-quality, inexpensive soda ash.

58
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:10,490
Modern soapmaking was born some 20 years later, in the early 19th century,

59
00:04:10,490 --> 00:04:17,612
with the discovery by Michel Eugène Chevreul, another French chemist, of the chemical nature

60
00:04:17,612 --> 00:04:21,410
and relationship of fats, glycerine and fatty acids.

61
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:26,814
His studies established the basis for both fat and soap chemistry,

62
00:04:26,814 --> 00:04:29,270
and soapmaking became a science.

63
00:04:29,270 --> 00:04:35,910
Further developments during the 19th century made it easier and cheaper to manufacture soap.

64
00:04:35,910 --> 00:04:41,043
Until the 19th century, soap was regarded as a luxury item,

65
00:04:41,043 --> 00:04:44,310
and was heavily taxed in several countries.

66
00:04:44,310 --> 00:04:49,240
As it became more readily available, it became an everyday necessity,

67
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:54,270
a development that was reinforced when the high tax was removed.

68
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:58,537
Soap was then something ordinary people could afford,

69
00:04:58,537 --> 00:05:00,670
and cleanliness standards improved.

70
00:05:00,670 --> 00:05:05,476
With this widespread use came the development of milder soaps for bathing

71
00:05:05,476 --> 00:05:08,680
and soaps for use in the washing machines

72
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:12,610
that were available to consumers by the turn of the 20th century.
